  <div class="section-label">Overview</div>
  <h2>Regional Mandates and Growth Pressure</h2>

  <p>The planning context for the SR-177 extension is governed by a tiered hierarchy of state and regional mandates, primarily channeled through UDOT and the Wasatch Front Regional Council. The overarching objective is to accommodate a projected population surge in western Davis and Weber counties, where the number of households is anticipated to increase by <strong>74% by 2050</strong>.{% cite "wfrc-rtp-2023-2050" %} This demographic pressure necessitates a high-capacity, limited-access arterial capable of diverting traffic from the increasingly saturated Interstate 15 corridor.</p>

  <p>The integration of SR-177 into the Wasatch Front Regional Council 2023–2050 Regional Transportation Plan{% cite "wfrc-rtp-2023-2050" %} ensures the project aligns with the "Wasatch Choice Vision,"{% cite "wfrc-wasatch-choice" %} which coordinates transportation infrastructure with land-use decisions and economic development strategies. For West Haven and Hooper, the highway is not merely a transit route but a catalyst for high-density residential rezoning and industrial expansion — specifically in relation to the Western Weber Inland Port project area.</p>

  <div class="callout">
    <p><strong>Commuter growth projections (UDOT):</strong> Daily commuter trips from western Weber County to Davis County are expected to grow from ~9,250 today to <strong>16,210 by 2050</strong>. Trips to Salt Lake County will increase from 2,310 to 5,370. Over 40% of workers in western Weber County currently commute to other counties.{% cite "udot-ses-build" %}</p>
  </div>

